## Test doubles

Style: {{DOUBLES_STYLE}}. Location: `{{DOUBLES_LOCATION}}`.

{{DOUBLES_RULE}}

Three doubles cover nearly everything:

- **success double** — in-memory, seedable, returns realistic data.
- **error double** — configurable to return a specific typed failure for the
  next call (a small control such as "fail next with NotFound"), rather than a
  per-test stubbed return value.
- **pending double** — does not complete until the test releases it, so an
  in-progress state can be asserted. Only where the unit has one.

**A fake is declared as the port type, never inferred from the fake.** The test
variable and the fake's own declaration name the port explicitly, so the
compiler checks the fake against the contract. A variable typed from the fake
compiles even after the fake has drifted from the real adapter, and the test
then proves nothing about production. {{FAKE_PORT_TYPING}} Controls the test
needs beyond the port are added alongside the port type, never instead of it.

**Shared ports have shared fakes.** Time, ids and delays come through ports; the
fakes to use are the ones listed in `{{ARCH_SKILL}}` → "Shared capability
ports" (a fixed or steppable clock, sequential ids, a manual scheduler the test
advances). Never patch global time — no fake-timer mode, no monkeypatched clock
— to work around a missing port. A missing port is a design fix, not a test
trick.

**Never fake:**

- the unit under test — testing a caller with a faked callee means you tested
  the fake;
- a use case — build the real one over faked driven ports;
- a state adapter — use the real one, it holds no logic.

**Seed state the way the app would.** Arrange a scenario through a use case or
by seeding the fake repository. Never call state setters directly to arrange a
scenario the unit could not reach, and never assert on state-holder internals.

**Fixtures are typed.** Build domain values through small fixture builders with
sensible defaults. If a fixture does not type-check, the builder is wrong — fix
it. Never silence it with a type escape hatch ({{TYPE_ESCAPE_HATCHES}}) or by
force-unwrapping a value that should have been valid.

## Structure

Given / When / Then, in that order, with blank lines between them. One behaviour
per test. If a test needs the word "and" to describe it, it is two tests.

Prefer one setup helper at the top of the file that builds the unit with its
doubles (a `makeSut`-style factory, fixture class or builder), so each test
differs only in what it is actually about.

## What to assert

- The **returned value** — the caller's contract.
- The **observable state change**, through the port's read side, if the unit
  owns one.
- The **typed error**, on the failure path — discriminate it by
  {{ERROR_DISCRIMINATE_BY}}. Never by a substring, prefix or equality of its
  message text.

Do not assert on internals, call counts of things that are not the contract, or
the behaviour of third-party libraries. Those assertions break on every refactor
and prove nothing.

## Testing driving adapters and presentation models

A driving adapter's test checks the adapter and nothing else. The use case it
calls already has its own tests.

**Build it for real.** Use real use cases with doubles for every driven port. Do
not mock the use case. To observe the calls the adapter makes, use a
**call-through spy**: {{SPY_TECHNIQUE}} The real code still runs, so the wiring
is exercised and a signature mismatch fails the test. The spy only records
*when* and *with what* the use case was called.

**Drive outcomes through the doubles.** To test an error branch, make the fake
port return the typed error. Never stub the use case's return value.

| Assert | Do not assert |
|---|---|
| The use case is called, with the right arguments | What the use case wrote to state/storage |
| It is called at the right moment: on the event not at startup, once not twice, after debounce not before | Which port method the use case called |
| It is **not** called: invalid input, disabled state, duplicate trigger, teardown | The domain result of a rule the use case applies |
| What the adapter exposes for each outcome (status code, exit code, view state — success and each error case) | Anything already asserted in the use case's test |
| The adapter's own logic: input parsing, error → transport/message mapping, loading/dialog flags | |

**Before writing an adapter test, find the use-case test for the same
behaviour.** If one exists, assert the *call*, not the *effect*. If none exists
and the behaviour is a business rule, the rule is in the wrong layer. Move it
inward (`separation-of-concerns`) and test it there.

Budget: about one interaction test per handler, plus one reaction test per
outcome the adapter handles differently. An adapter test file larger than the
tests of the use cases it calls is a sign that it is re-testing them.

